(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_animalkingdom=[{id:"neet-2026r-q093",pn:93,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Reptilia characteristics",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I : The class name Reptilia refers to creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.
Statement II : All organisms belonging to Reptilia have three chambered heart.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`The name Reptilia refers to their creeping or crawling mode of locomotion.
The heart is usually three-chambered, but four-chambered in crocodiles.
So not all reptiles have a three-chambered heart.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q104",pn:104,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Characters of chordates",q:"Which of the following is not a characteristic of chordates?",o:["Central nervous system is dorsal","Absence of gills","Presence of post anal part (tail)","Presence of notochord"],c:1,s:`Members of phylum Chordata show these features at some stage of life:
Presence of notochord.
Pharynx perforated by gill slits.
Dorsal, hollow and single central nervous system.
Ventral heart.
A post-anal tail.
Gill slits are absent in non-chordates, so 'absence of gills' is not a chordate character.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q133",pn:133,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Symmetry in animals",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : When any plane passing through the central axis of the body divides the organism into two identical halves, it is called radial symmetry.
Statement II : In phylum Echinodermata, both adults and larvae are radially symmetrical.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:1,s:`Statement I is correct: a plane through the central axis giving two identical halves means radial symmetry.
Echinoderms show radial or bilateral symmetry depending on the stage.
Adults are radially symmetrical, but larvae are bilaterally symmetrical.
So Statement II is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2026-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Class Cyclostomata",q:`A group of researchers procured some fish like animals and upon investigation the following characters were observed:
A. Endoskeleton was made of cartilage.
B. Ectoparasitic; as they were found attached on fish skin with their circular sucking mouth.
C. Paired fins and scales were absent, but 7 pairs of gill slits were present.
Which of the following species of animals did they consider to fit best with these characters?`,o:["Exocoetus sp.","Branchiostoma sp.","Petromyzon sp.","Scoliodon sp"],c:2,s:`Petromyzon has a cartilaginous endoskeleton.
It has a circular sucking mouth and is an ectoparasite on some fishes.
Its body has no scales and no paired fins.
It has 7 pairs (6-15 pairs) of gill slits.
Scoliodon and Exocoetus are not parasites.`},{id:"neet-2026-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Aves: flightless birds",q:"The flightless bird with forelimbs modified as paddle-like structures suited for swimming is known as:",o:["Struthio","Psittacula","Neophron","Aptenodytes"],c:3,s:`Neophron is a vulture and Psittacula is a parrot. Both fly.
Struthio is an ostrich and Aptenodytes is a penguin. Both are flightless.
In penguins the forelimbs are modified into flippers (paddle-like) used for swimming.
In ostriches the forelimbs are small and used for balance while running, not for swimming.`},{id:"neet-2026-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Class Osteichthyes",q:"Select the set of fishes which belong to the class Osteichthyes:",o:["Devil fish, Cuttlefish and Hagfish","Starfish, Hagfish and Cuttlefish","Flying fish, Angel fish and Fighting fish","Saw fish, Fighting fish and Dog fish"],c:2,s:`Option 3 is correct.
Flying fish (Exocoetus) is a marine bony fish. Angel fish (Pterophyllum) and fighting fish (Betta) are aquarium bony fishes.
Option 1 is wrong: devil fish (Octopus) and cuttlefish (Sepia) are molluscs. Hag fish (Myxine) is a cyclostome.
Option 4 is wrong: saw fish (Pristis) and dog fish (Scoliodon) are cartilaginous fishes.
Option 2 is wrong: starfish (Asterias) is an echinoderm.`},{id:"neet-2026-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Haploid cells and gametogenesis",q:"In which animal do haploid cells divide mitotically to produce gametes?",o:["Male honeybees","Male grasshoppers","Male earthworms","Male frogs"],c:0,s:`The male honeybee is haploid and the female is diploid.
Gamete formation in female honeybees is by meiosis, whereas male honeybees form gametes by mitosis.
Thus haploid cells undergo mitosis in male honeybees.`},{id:"neet-2026-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Phyla characteristics",q:`Select the incorrect statements from the following:
A. Digestive system in Platyhelminthes is incomplete.
B. Bilateral symmetry is a characteristic feature of adult Echinoderms.
C. Pseudocoelom is possessed by Aschelminthes.
D. Notochord is persistent throughout life in the class Chondrichthyes.
E. Members of class Reptilia maintain a constant body temperature.
Choose the answer from the options given below:`,o:["B and E only","C and D only","A and C only","B and D only"],c:0,s:`A is correct: Platyhelminthes have an incomplete digestive system.
B is incorrect: bilateral symmetry is a feature of echinoderm larvae. Adult echinoderms show radial symmetry.
C is correct: Aschelminthes have a pseudocoelom.
D is correct: the notochord is persistent throughout life in Chondrichthyes.
E is incorrect: reptiles are cold-blooded, so they cannot maintain a constant body temperature. Warm-blooded birds and mammals can.
The incorrect statements are B and E only.`},{id:"neet-2025-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Types of coelom",q:"While trying to find out the characteristic of a newly found animal, a researcher did the histology of adult animal and observed a cavity with presence of mesodermal tissue towards the body wall but no mesodermal tissue was observed towards the alimentary canal. What could be the possible coelome of that animal?",o:["Acoelomate","Pseudocoelomate","Schizocoelomate","Spongocoelomate"],c:1,s:`In pseudocoelomates the body cavity is not entirely lined with mesoderm.
Mesodermal tissue is present along the body wall but not towards the gut.
Schizocoelomates have a coelom that develops from a split in the mesoderm.
In acoelomates the coelom is absent.
Spongocoel is a central cavity found in sponges.`},{id:"neet-2025-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Class Cyclostomata",q:"All living members of the class Cyclostomata are :",o:["Free living","Endoparasite","Symbiotic","Ectoparasite"],c:3,s:"All living members of class Cyclostomata are ectoparasites."},{id:"neet-2025-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Phylum Chordata, vertebrates",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : All vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrate.
Reason (R) : The members of subphylum vertebrata possess notochord during the embryonic period, the notochord is replaced by cartilaginous or bony vertebral column in adults.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:0,s:`Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) explains (A).
Members of subphylum Vertebrata have a notochord in the embryonic period.
In adults the notochord is replaced by a cartilaginous or bony vertebral column.
Thus all vertebrates are chordates but all chordates are not vertebrates.`},{id:"neet-2025-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Echinoderms",q:`Role of the water vascular system in Echinoderms is:
A. Respiration and Locomotion
B. Excretion and Locomotion
C. Capture and transport of food
D. Digestion and Respiration
E. Digestion and Excretion
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and B Only","A and C Only","B and C Only","B, D and E Only"],c:1,s:`The water vascular system of echinoderms helps in locomotion, capture and transport of food, and respiration.
Echinoderms have no excretory system. Excretion takes place through the general body surface.`},{id:"neet-2024-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Body cavity types",q:`Consider the following statements :
A. Annelids are true coelomates
B. Poriferans are pseudocoelomates
C. Aschelminthes are acoelomates
D. Platyhelminthes are pseudocoelomates
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["C only","D only","B only","A only"],c:3,s:`Option (4) is correct: annelids are true coelomates.
Poriferans are acoelomates, aschelminthes are pseudocoelomates and platyhelminthes are acoelomates.
So B, C and D are wrong.`},{id:"neet-2024-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Phylum characteristics",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Pleurobrachia \u2014 I. Mollusca
B. Radule \u2014 II. Ctenophora
C. Stomochord \u2014 III. Osteichthyes
D. Air bladder \u2014 IV. Hemichordata`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],c:3,s:`Option (4) is correct.
A. Pleurobrachia belongs to phylum Ctenophora.
B. Radula is a rasping feeding organ of Mollusca.
C. Stomochord is a small notochord-like structure in the collar region of Hemichordata.
D. Air bladder is found in Osteichthyes and gives buoyancy.`},{id:"neet-2024-q182",pn:182,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Fishes: scientific and common names",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. Pterophyllum \u2014 I. Hag fish
B. Myxine \u2014 II. Saw fish
C. Pristis \u2014 III. Angel fish
D. Exocoetus \u2014 IV. Flying fish`,o:["A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],c:3,s:`Pterophyllum is the angel fish.
Myxine is the hag fish.
Pristis is the saw fish.
Exocoetus is the flying fish.
So A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV.
Note: The printed answer is [4], which is correct; the printed solution says option (2), a misprint.`},{id:"neet-2024-q186",pn:186,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Chordates vs non-chordates",q:`The following are the statements about non-chordates:
A. Pharynx is perforated by gill slits.
B. Notochord is absent.
C. Central nervous system is dorsal.
D. Heart is dorsal if present.
E. Post anal tail is absent.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["B, D & E only","B, C & D only","A & C only","A, B & D only"],c:0,s:`Non-chordate features among these: B (notochord absent), D (heart dorsal if present), E (post-anal tail absent).
A and C are features of chordates.
So option (1).`},{id:"neet-2023-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Body symmetry",q:"Radial symmetry is NOT found in adults of phylum ___________ .",o:["Echinodermata","Ctenophora","Hemichordata","Coelenterata"],c:2,s:`Hemichordates are bilaterally symmetrical.
Coelenterates and ctenophores are radially symmetrical.
Adult echinoderms are also radially symmetrical.`},{id:"neet-2023-q194",pn:194,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Mammalian characteristics",q:"The unique mammalian characteristics are:",o:["pinna, monocondylic skull and mammary glands","hairs, tympanic membrane and mammary glands","hairs, pinna and mammary glands","hairs, pinna and indirect development"],c:2,s:`Hairs, pinna and mammary glands are unique to mammals.
Monocondylic skull is seen in reptiles and birds; mammals have a dicondylic skull.
Tympanic membrane is also present in amphibians.
Mammals do not show indirect development.`},{id:"neet-2023-q196",pn:196,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Chordate characters",q:`Select the correct statements with reference to chordates.
A. Presence of a mid-dorsal, solid and double nerve cord.
B. Presence of closed circulatory system.
C. Presence of paired pharyngeal gill slits.
D. Presence of dorsal heart
E. Triploblastic pseudocoelomate animals.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["C, D and E only","A, C and D only","B and C only","B, D and E only"],c:2,s:`Chordates have a closed circulatory system and paired pharyngeal gill slits. So B and C are correct.
Their nerve cord is dorsal, hollow and single, so A is wrong.
The heart is ventral, so D is wrong.
They are triploblastic and coelomate, so E is wrong.`}]})();
